          <Pad Id="01Kdq3EMv71CIPBmU875pm" Bounds="809,9768,589,46" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="39. Records &amp; Operations">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">22</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="12N0SVaK5sowlLdiQhKLoh" Bounds="1701,4840,397,207" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="COMMENTS&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;You pick a Process when you:&#xD;&#xA;- want to create modules of your patches for a better overview&#xD;&#xA;- want to create abstract functionality into re-usable components&#xD;&#xA;- want to split your application into several documents, for example when working as a team&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;A process is an object and can be created and used several times&#xD;&#xA;Has to be placed manually in the patch&#xD;&#xA;What if we could create objects dynamically during the runtime?">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="6KW1LVyR3RetLqnNDNkS6l" Bounds="772,4028,414,259" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="Almost since we started patching we have been working with objects in vvvv. Every Circle, every Rectangle, every Material, every Spread you have placed in your patch is an instance of a data type that someone defined somewhere. And from chapter 11 onwards you have also started defining your own — every Process node is in fact a little object, with its own pins, its own behavior, and its own little life inside the patch.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;In this final part of the tutorial we will name what you have been doing all along, and then unlock the rest of it. From here on you will be able to define your own data types from the ground up, give them properties, give them behavior, and use them to organize your patches in ways that were not possible before.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="3CHUYUZjBSYIgKlAMC7QWc" Bounds="1240,4042,300,31" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="What is an Object">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">15</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="5N3gDlsEzwnoMKdxyTVrUc" Bounds="1240,4084,403,202" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="An object in programming is a data type that bundles two things together — the data it holds and the operations that work on it. &#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;You can see an object as a composition of the primitive data types you have got to know so far. An object ist basically a combination of value types to come up with something more complex. In this sense a Vector2 is already an object. A Vector2 (Join) takes an X and a Y and bundles them into one type. A Vector2 (Split) takes that bundle apart again. The two values travel together as one thing, with their own name and their own identity in the patch.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="6KdjT7UwyQeJJaNTMnLRlZ" Bounds="1699,4024,300,31" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="Defining Your Own Object">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">15</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="6A5L8IcW6uxjxyrlInSVyo" Bounds="1699,4066,403,334" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="Remember the ColoredCircle from chapter 11. We defined it as a Process node — it had input pins for Position, Size and Color, an Update operation that drew the circle, and we placed it three times in the patch to draw three different circles.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;That ColoredCircle was already an object. We did not call it that at the time, but bundling properties and behavior into a named type with a place in the Node Browser is exactly what an object is. Every Process node you have written in this tutorial has been your own custom data type, with its own bundle of pins and its own little life.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;So we are not starting from zero in this part of the tutorial. We are taking what you can already do and going one step further — from defining types as Process nodes to defining them as Records, which is the default and most flexible way to define a data type in VL.&#xD;&#xA;">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="5dhQtOJiktkVLzbPMgBgyo" Bounds="2140,4020,300,31" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="A Record From Scratch">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">15</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="6t7AiJuBKb4LZQLKAU0l6o" Bounds="2140,4062,400,304" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="A Record is the default way to define your own data type in VL. You can create one by typing the name you want in the Node Browser and choosing &quot;Record&quot;. You can also create one directly from the Patch Explorer in the top-left corner of the document.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Once you have a Record, you can give it properties. A property is a named value of any type — a Float, a String, a Vector, even another Record. Every property becomes a pin on the Create operation, so you can set its value when you make a new instance.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;In the example below we have defined a Record called Thing. It has two properties — a Position and a Size. Right-click on the Thing region in the Patch Explorer to open its definition, then come back and look at the Create operation. The two properties show up as input pins on the left, and the Thing itself comes out on the right.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="0AUstWvjftQ3ZQentXd8Ba" Bounds="1240,4699,404,77" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="**Live element (x=92, y=720):** Small dry demonstration — a Circle node with values feeding in, and a Hit Test operation showing the result. Just enough to make &quot;data + operations bundled together&quot; concrete. Two IOBoxes for the inputs, one for the output. The reader can hover and see &quot;Circle&quot; in the tooltip.&#xD;&#xA;">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">7</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="3CIDsWJlH8Qm0O3sS7ugzY" Bounds="2140,4376,399,240" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="**Live element (x=1092, y=750):** A minimal Record called `Thing` with two properties — `Position` (Vector2) and `Size` (Float). Its Create operation is shown in the application patch with two IOBoxes feeding its inputs and one IOBox reading its output. The output IOBox just shows &quot;Thing&quot; — the reader sees that the Record exists as a type without yet seeing what's inside it.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;The Record definition itself sits in the Patch Explorer; the reader is instructed to open it via right-click.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;**Critical pedagogical choice:** the Record is deliberately *not* Particle. It is `Thing` — the dryest, most obviously-a-placeholder name we can give it. This signals to the reader that the Record itself is not the point of the chapter; the *concept* of &quot;defining a type with properties&quot; is. Chapter 39 will introduce a more substantial Record. Chapter 41 will introduce the Particle as the carrying example through Part V's use cases.&#xD;&#xA;">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">7</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="4LxIkIxQM7LcdndkxHikbh" Bounds="2593,4006,300,31" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="Inspecting Your Record">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">15</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="07sQImyczeKqB58fNkIX7X" Bounds="2593,4048,400,181" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="One of the nicest things about defining your own data type is that you can inspect it from anywhere in the patch, just like any built-in type. Hover over a link carrying a Thing and you will see the type name in the tooltip. Right-click on the Thing region in the Patch Explorer and you can see all its properties at a glance. As you go through the rest of this tutorial, this becomes one of the main ways to understand what is happening in your patch — every link carries a value of a known type, and every value can be inspected.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="2PUc5cFLEkTxMiVruTiVAs" Bounds="2593,4244,401,78" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="**Live element (x=92, y=1010):** The reader sees the `Thing` from column 3 placed in a larger inspection layout. A few additional IOBoxes labeled to suggest &quot;hover here to see Thing in the tooltip&quot; — basically annotated waypoints. This is just an invitation to interact with the patch via hovering, not a new lesson.&#xD;&#xA;">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">7</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="5HETAfopCPI8kB29Lz4skB" Bounds="3040,4008,410,31" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="Records, Classes, and What's Coming">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">15</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="1whVPfsol60M6pkcUABsY1" Bounds="3040,4050,397,368" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="Record is the default way to define your own data type in VL, and it is what we will use throughout most of Part V. There is also a second flavor called Class, which is the same idea with one specific difference — Classes can be changed in place, while Records always produce a new copy when you modify them. We will get to Classes in chapter 42, where the difference will become visible and useful. Until then, every example we build will be a Record.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;In the next chapter we will give a Record real behavior. We will define our own operations — setters, getters, anything we want — and see how a Record with custom operations becomes a properly useful little thing. From there we will look at two use cases that show why working with Records makes patches dramatically cleaner than what we have been doing so far.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="7LiqmPRbuth9pTeERvGtfB" Bounds="92,4142,488,606" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="**1. The Thing-not-Particle choice.** A deliberate placeholder name signals to the reader that the type is incidental — what matters is the *act of defining*. If chapter 38 introduces the Particle, the chapter starts carrying narrative weight that should belong to chapter 41 (Use Case II), which is where the Particle should arrive as the carrying example.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;**2. Two back-references, no more.** The chapter explicitly names chapter 10 (Circle) and chapter 11 (ColoredCircle / Process nodes). Both are load-bearing because the chapter's central claim is &quot;you have been using and defining objects all along.&quot; Other earlier chapters could be mentioned, but two clean anchors are stronger than a list.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;**3. The &quot;Defining Your Own&quot; column is the conceptual hinge.** It explicitly says Process nodes are objects. This is the single most important move in the chapter — without it, Part V reads as &quot;now we learn something new&quot;; with it, Part V reads as &quot;now we name what we have been doing.&quot; That's the framing the whole section depends on.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;**4. The forward pointer to Class is one paragraph in the closing.** No earlier mention. The chapter resists the temptation to set up Class as a coming attraction throughout — it just plants it at the end. This matches how chapter 24 mentions the broader operations concept at its end without trying to teach it.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;**5. Mutability not mentioned by name.** The forward pointer to Class talks about &quot;changed in place&quot; vs &quot;produces a new copy&quot; without using the words mutable or immutable. The vocabulary is held back for chapter 42, where it gets its proper introduction. This keeps chapter 38's surface vocabulary smaller.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;**6. Length:** Roughly four columns of body text plus two live patches. Comparable to chapter 11 (Process Nodes), which is the closest precedent in scope and ambition.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Overlay Id="5rFTKZoBY4bThhiYbbZ95O" Name="" Bounds="805,9567,4576,80">
            <p:ColorIndex p:Type="Int32">4</p:ColorIndex>
          </Overlay>
          <Pad Id="2DUCIVMbf1Sxz1ggAVdGZX" Bounds="92,4025,516,95" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="The recognition chapter. Names what the reader has been doing since chapter 10 (using objects) and chapter 11 (defining objects). Introduces Record as the default way to define your own data type in VL. Plants the smallest possible Record so the chapter has a working patch. Mentions Class only as a forward pointer to chapter 42.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="6UxoqYS44R5WySQTsL0X62" Bounds="107,9910,540,908" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="**1. The Thing is carried forward from chapter 38, deliberately.** Chapter 38 ended with a Record called Thing with two properties. Chapter 39 picks up that same Thing and adds operations. This reinforces the &quot;we are not starting fresh; we are deepening&quot; feel that Part V wants throughout. The Particle still doesn't appear — it's reserved for chapter 41.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;**2. The State Input / State Output visual cue.** Introduced here as just a description (&quot;not visually connected&quot;), not yet weighted as a teaching point. By chapter 42 the reader will have seen this pin behavior in many examples and will recognize the visual immediately when the dashed-link variant appears for Classes. This is deliberate priming — letting a visual idiom become familiar before being named.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;**3. The &quot;every operation produces a new copy&quot; idea.** This is the core mechanic but it is taught indirectly — through hovering and tooltip inspection rather than through a stated rule. The reader builds the intuition by experiencing the patch, which is stronger than memorizing a statement. The naming as &quot;mutability&quot; or &quot;immutability&quot; is reserved for chapter 42.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;**4. Setters and getters as a pair.** This is the conventional terminology and worth using because it transfers cleanly to other programming contexts the reader may encounter. The chapter does not labor the terms — it names them, uses them, and moves on. The vocabulary is teachable in passing because it's self-explanatory.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;**5. Static vs member operations gets a column but no live example.** A small choice — the column is descriptive, naming the distinction and giving the reader enough to recognize it. A worked example would require introducing a second type, which adds complexity for limited gain. The reader who needs more can follow the Gray Book link. This matches how chapter 11 handled certain mechanical details — name them, describe them, link out for depth.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;**6. The forward pointer to chapter 44.** One column at the bottom-middle, brief and honest. The reader is told that Process-enabling exists but that Part V will work with manually-called operations until chapter 44. This frames the rest of Part V correctly — you're not missing something; you're learning the foundation that chapter 44 will then synthesize.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;**7. Length.** Roughly six columns of body text plus four live patches. Slightly longer than chapter 38, which is appropriate because this is the deeper mechanics chapter. Comparable in scope to chapter 24 (Update &amp; Create) which it directly extends.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;**8. What this chapter does *not* do.** No mention of Dispose (saved for chapter 41 and properly demonstrated in chapter 44). No mention of mutability or Classes by name beyond chapter 38's closing forward pointer. No mention of the Apply pin pattern from chapters 23/25/26 — that's a different mechanism and conflating it here would muddy the operation-as-node story. The reader meets the Apply pin's relevance to custom operations naturally when they get there in chapter 41 (Use Case II).">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="1Wm1Yj3cRoHTKty16KHB1E" Bounds="107,9771,515,120" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="The mechanics chapter for Records. Takes the Thing from chapter 38 and gives it real behavior — custom operations, setters, getters. Introduces the static/member operation distinction. Shows the value-flow-through-tooltips story (every step copies the Record forward). Closes with a forward pointer to chapter 44 (Objects as Process Nodes) where operations can be made to run automatically.&#xD;&#xA;">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="1s3NWAtslB77EnnWIzdqT2" Bounds="809,9874,401,459" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="In the last chapter we defined our first Record — a Thing with two properties. So far that Thing does nothing. It just exists. In this chapter we will give it behavior. We will look at the default operations every Record gets automatically, and then we will define our own.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;A Record's operations are its verbs. They are the named things you can do to it — make a new one, read its properties, change them, calculate something based on them. Each operation appears as its own node in the patch, with its own pins, and every operation that modifies the Record produces a new copy with the changes applied. This will become important when you look at how data flows through your patch using only your custom type.&#xD;&#xA;">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="7cDWtv7BqXl9qneJiV4p1v" Bounds="1277,9888,349,31" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="Default Operations">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">15</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="3ReOQLv6GlGfeVMAWfXsQd" Bounds="1277,9930,401,330" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="Every Record you define comes with two operations automatically — Create and Split.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Create makes a new instance of your Record. Every property you defined appears as an input pin, and the new Record comes out on the right. This is how you bring a Record into existence in the patch. You can use Create as many times as you want — each call produces a separate instance with its own data.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Split is the opposite. Give it a Record, and it gives you all the properties back as separate outputs. This is how you read what's inside a Record at any point in your patch. The Record itself stays unchanged — Split only reads it.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Both operations show up as nodes when you start typing the name of your Record in the Node Browser. Try it in the patch below.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="54oNwSwzA7EJNGr1E7oLgr" Bounds="1277,10255,401,186" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="**Live element (x=92, y=750):**&#xD;&#xA;- The `Thing` from chapter 38 (now reused — the reader has met it).&#xD;&#xA;- A `Create Thing` node with two IOBoxes for Position and Size.&#xD;&#xA;- A `Split Thing` node downstream, with two output IOBoxes reading the Position and Size back out.&#xD;&#xA;- The link between Create and Split is a solid white line; hovering on it shows &quot;Thing&quot; in the tooltip.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;**Pedagogical note:** Create and Split show the symmetric &quot;into&quot; and &quot;out of&quot; pattern. The chapter does not yet introduce mutation — that's the next column.&#xD;&#xA;">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">7</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="2uc20LXeyXu6m3rF2R4YJa" Bounds="1759,9861,349,31" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="Defining a Custom Operation">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">15</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="1W0qSCkT3c9nH8UcwsDS8m" Bounds="1759,9903,407,427" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="Default operations are useful but limited. To give a Record real behavior we define our own operations. Open the Patch Explorer, right-click your Record and choose &quot;Add Operation&quot;. You will be asked to name it. Let's call ours SetPosition.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Inside the operation definition you will find a State Input pin on the left and a State Output pin on the right. The State Input is the Record coming in — the Thing as it exists right now. The State Output is the Record going out — the new Thing that this operation produces. In between, you patch whatever logic you want.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;To change the Position, we patch a Vector2 input pin and assign its value to the Position property of the State Output. The operation now has two inputs — the State Input on the left (the Thing) and a Vector2 from above (the new Position). It has one output — the modified Thing.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Notice that the State Input and the State Output are not visually connected to each other. That is intentional. It tells you that the Thing coming out is not the same Thing that went in — it is a fresh copy with the new Position applied. The original Thing on the left is unchanged.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="1YjnIgGRUDtP9BWp5D42mh" Bounds="1759,10353,401,186" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="**Live element (x=592, y=920):**&#xD;&#xA;- The `Thing` is now extended with a custom operation `SetPosition`.&#xD;&#xA;- The application patch shows: `Create Thing` → `SetPosition` (with a Vector2 input from a slider) → an IOBox or downstream Split to verify the new position.&#xD;&#xA;- The links carrying Thing are solid.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;**Pedagogical note:** The &quot;State Input and State Output not visually connected&quot; detail is the seed of mutability understanding. The chapter does not name it as such — that vocabulary is reserved for chapter 42. But the visual cue is being planted here, where the reader meets it for the first time, so it has time to become familiar before it is named.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">7</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="61PYo92mHR2XuDBW7sP2HZ" Bounds="2207,9865,349,31" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="Setters and Getters">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">15</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="43EM08ERV6v7aUCbrIK9gP" Bounds="2207,9907,399,397" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="SetPosition is what's called a setter — an operation that changes one property of the Record and returns the modified version. You can define a setter for every property you want to expose: SetSize, SetColor, SetSpeed, anything.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;The mirror image of a setter is a getter — an operation that reads a property without changing the Record. GetPosition takes a Thing in on State Input, reads its Position property, and outputs that Position on its own pin. The State Output sends the same Thing back out, unchanged. You did not modify anything — you only read.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;You will notice that getters do something Split also does — they read property values. The difference is convenience. Split gives you all properties at once. A getter gives you just one, with a clear name, and is useful when you only need one value at a time or when the calculation is more complex than just reading.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;For our Thing, we have defined SetPosition, SetSize, GetPosition, and GetSize. Each one shows up in the Node Browser as a separate node, and you can place them anywhere in your patch.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="133uNXUdSI2a472BFIITtJ" Bounds="2207,10342,399,100" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="**Live element (x=1092, y=970):**&#xD;&#xA;- A chain showing `Create Thing` → `SetPosition` → `SetSize` → `GetPosition` (output value) with `GetSize` tapped in further downstream.&#xD;&#xA;- IOBoxes feeding each setter.&#xD;&#xA;- Output IOBoxes showing the values from the getters.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">7</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="1s4J6eZdexP6qsm4ysUHeF" Bounds="2669,9877,349,31" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="Inspecting the Flow">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">15</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="6nYRUqvySAwm7C5lcWsPqS" Bounds="2669,9919,399,397" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="One of the nicest features of working with a custom Record is that you can inspect the value at any point along its journey through your patch. Hover over any link carrying a Thing and the tooltip shows the type name. Right-click and you can pin a tooltip in place to keep watching the value as it flows through.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;In the chain below we run a Thing through a sequence of operations — SetPosition, then SetSize, then SetPosition again. After each operation a new copy of the Thing is produced with the changes applied. The earlier Things still exist for as long as the patch needs them, but downstream you see only the latest version.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Try hovering at different points along the chain to see how the Thing evolves. You can compare any two points by pinning their tooltips. This is the same technique you would use to debug any value in vvvv — Records are not special in this regard, which is exactly the point.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="7DrN678Veemreh4SSYtFr9" Bounds="2671,10285,399,100" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="**Live element (x=92, y=1250):** A wide horizontal patch — `Create Thing` → `SetPosition` → `SetSize` → `SetPosition` → final Split. IOBoxes for each setter's secondary input (Vector2 or Float). Annotation IOBoxes labeled &quot;hover here&quot; at three or four points along the chain. The pedagogical move is *handing the reader a tool*, not teaching a new concept.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">7</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="1Mgsh7LgbtWG3FVk1SBqbn" Bounds="3139,9871,349,31" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="Static and Member Operations">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">15</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="13VwuU8ijlpZyAWX1pIW9P" Bounds="3139,9913,397,428" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="The operations we have defined so far are called member operations. They belong to a specific data type — SetPosition belongs to Thing. You cannot use SetPosition on a Circle or a Rectangle; it only knows how to work with a Thing.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;There is a second kind of operation called a static operation. A static operation does not belong to any one data type. It just takes inputs, does something, and produces outputs. The math nodes you have been using since chapter 3 — Add, Multiply, Sin, Cos — are all static operations. They work on any compatible value you feed them.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Static operations are defined directly in the Patch Explorer, separate from any Record. You give them a name, define their inputs and outputs, and they show up in the Node Browser like any other utility node.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;When should you define one or the other? Member operations are for behavior that belongs to a specific type. Static operations are for general utilities that work across many types. If you find yourself writing the same calculation for two different Records, that calculation probably wants to be a static operation.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="0wZ2nfqXKRFd2OkBn8XfoL" Bounds="3142,10350,399,100" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="**Live element (x=92, y=1250):** A static operation">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">7</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="56UNEmRPswrpFpZmcEffLr" Bounds="3614,9871,349,31" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="A Glimpse Ahead">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">15</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="0zXeQQ3bYrPQMulTbz4Xxl" Bounds="3614,9913,392,292" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="Every time you place an operation in the patch, you call it manually — the operation runs when its inputs change or when you wire it into your update flow. In chapter 44 we will see that there is also a mode where the operations of a Record (or Class) can run automatically — Create when the Record is placed in the patch, Update every frame, and so on. That mode makes a Record behave like a Process node.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;We are not enabling that mode yet. For Part V we will work with Records the way we have here — defining operations and calling them ourselves. This gives us the most control and the clearest picture of what is happening. Chapter 44 will pull that together at the end.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
